 # ---- SECONDARY Health and wellbeing: Wellbeing online
 ("RSHE-S-WO-1", ("secondary","Wellbeing online"), 1, "FULL",
  "ks3-10-mood-and-screens",
  "ks3-10 teaches the mood audit (better, worse or nothing) and the research position honestly: the link between raw hours and wellbeing is real but tiny, and what matters is what the time is made of.",
  ""),
 ("RSHE-S-WO-2", ("secondary","Wellbeing online"), 2, "FULL",
  "ks3-10-mood-and-screens; ks3-14-bodies-image-pressure",
  "ks3-10 defines comparing as 'Measuring your real, unedited life against someone else's chosen highlights' and sets it against connecting. ks3-14 covers body image and edited content.",
  "Closed by migration 309. ks3-10 now asks the class which of their connections would survive the app closing, which is the over reliance question in the form a fourteen year old will actually answer."),
 ("RSHE-S-WO-3", ("secondary","Wellbeing online"), 3, "FULL",
  "ks3-27-when-it-turns-on-you; ks4-29-did-not-go-looking",
  "ks3-27 teaches identifying the four named behaviours and the routes: report and block behind three dots, the safeguarding lead by name, Childline on 0800 1111. ks4-29 teaches the same routes for content that arrives unasked, including Report Harmful Content and Samaritans, and that there is no time limit on telling somebody.",
  "Closed by migrations 313 and 315. Support after being affected is taught in ks4-29 s12 'What helps afterwards': involuntary re seeing is an ordinary response, saying it out loud to one person shortens it, and the free routes are given with hours and ages."),
 ("RSHE-S-WO-4", ("secondary","Wellbeing online"), 4, "FULL",
  "ks4-28-the-money-and-the-odds; ks2-05-gaming-time-spend",
  "ks4-28 is a 31 slide module. s6 'The price you actually paid' and s8 'Two conversions and a leftover' teach how a game currency hides price and why the pack never divides into the crate. s9 'What you actually bought' names paying for a chance. s11 'The odds, and who set them' teaches the house edge from a roulette wheel. s17 'The loop, and how fast it runs' teaches speed as the risk, with the 2025 stake caps. s18 'What it does to a person' teaches the debt: money gone before the feeling of spending, borrowing, then hiding the borrowing.",
  "Closed by migration 314. The accumulation of debt clause is taught as mechanism rather than figure, because no reliable UK figure exists for under eighteens, and the teacher notes say so."),
 ("RSHE-S-WO-5", ("secondary","Wellbeing online"), 5, "FULL",
  "ks3-12-misinfo-deepfakes; ks4-15-manipulation-persuasion",
  "ks3-12 teaches misinformation and disinformation with distinct definitions. ks4-15 teaches targeting and the persuasion industry.",
  "Closed by migration 309. ks3-12 now teaches the conspiracy theory as a third shape with a tell: it is built so that evidence against it counts as proof of the cover up, and a claim nothing can disprove is unfalsifiable rather than strong. Deliberately no worked example, because naming a live theory in a classroom hands it an audience."),
 ("RSHE-S-WO-6", ("secondary","Wellbeing online"), 6, "FULL",
  "ks4-29-did-not-go-looking",
  "ks4-29 s17 'What the offer actually is' teaches recruitment into drug supply as a friendly offer from somebody half known that works like a debt, and knife supply as a website that is not a loophole: selling to under 18s has been an offence for decades and since 2022 delivering one bought online to a home address is too. s18 checks that money out of proportion to the task is the sign. s19 'The part of this that is on your side' states that supplying is a serious offence, that the offer alone completes it, that being sent a message is not an offence while passing it on can be, and that there is a defence for under 18s who were exploited, which does not stop arrest.",
  "Closed by migration 315. County lines is a keyword on s5. The defence and its limits are stated in the same breath, because a pupil told the comforting version and then arrested will decide the teacher lied."),
 ("RSHE-S-WO-7", ("secondary","Wellbeing online"), 7, "FULL",
  "ks4-29-did-not-go-looking",
  "ks4-29 s6 'Why it found you' teaches that a feed optimising for attention reaches distressing content without anyone choosing it, with the OxWell figure: about a third of pupils aged 11 to 18 in England saw self harm content in the past month, most without looking. s8 'You are not in trouble' is the spine. s9 'What it does to look' states the risk accurately: most are not harmed, repetition normalises, and that matters most for whoever is already struggling. s12 'What helps afterwards' gives Samaritans 116 123, Childline 0800 1111 and HOPELINE247 0800 068 4141. s21 'Stop it, report it, say it' is the reporting route with Report Harmful Content as the escalation.",
  "Closed by migration 315. Written inside Samaritans guidance: no method is named or described anywhere in the module. The practise sort puts the same violent video in two rows, seen once and still seen three days later, because what to do depends on what it is doing to you."),

